  "forest_plan": {
    "base_commit": "4fae7323a87b91ecf1b3dee948ce9a88560f2193",
    "children": [
      {
        "objective": "Explore conventional block-local source tiling while keeping one target particle per thread and serial traversal of the source dimension, so the main change is reusing staged source particles across output threads. Tune the tile layout and size, block configuration, and dynamic shared-memory setting within the stated launch limits. Handle arbitrary n and tail tiles without extra kernels, buffers, or host preprocessing. Preserve the entry point, Particle layout, complete outputs and masses; pass every supplied correctness case. Evaluate complete source-and-launch candidates using the official search measurements.",
        "role": "T1",
        "subject": "Shared-memory source tiling"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Explore register-level output coarsening: have each thread maintain multiple target particles and reuse each loaded source particle across those targets, without shared-memory source staging as the principal mechanism. Tune coarsening and launch parameters while handling all n and tails safely. Preserve the entry point, Particle layout, update order, complete output and exact masses; pass every supplied correctness case. Compare measured source-and-launch candidates under the official protocol.",
        "role": "T2",
        "subject": "Per-thread multi-target coarsening"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Explore a distinct mapping that assigns lanes within a warp (or block) to portions of the source-particle reduction for a target, then combines partial force sums. Focus on parallelizing the reduction dimension rather than output coarsening or block-wide source tiling. Handle arbitrary n, self-interaction exclusion, tail sources and reduction synchronization without races or extra launches/buffers. Preserve the entry point, Particle layout, integration order, and exact masses; validate all supplied cases and compare official measurements.",
        "role": "T3",
        "subject": "Source-parallel force reduction"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Explore per-interaction arithmetic reductions such as safe factoring of the target-mass term, reciprocal-square-root use, or other instruction simplifications compatible with NVRTC CUDA 12.6.3 and no global fast-math option. Keep self-interaction exclusion unless an alternative is proven safe, preserve float32 state and exact masses, and reject transformations that fail the FP64-oracle tolerances or produce nonfinite output—including on the isolated extreme-mass case. Keep the entry point and single-kernel interface; measure complete source-and-launch candidates using the official protocol.",
        "role": "T4",
        "subject": "Force arithmetic simplification"
      }
    ],
